WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.768 --> 00:00:11.893
The Sun is just one of hundreds
of billions of stars in our galaxy.

00:00:12.678 --> 00:00:15.428
In a way, there’s nothing
very special about it.

00:00:15.957 --> 00:00:17.749
It’s a fairly ordinary star -

00:00:17.941 --> 00:00:21.232
not particularly large or bright
by stellar standards.

00:00:21.474 --> 00:00:23.557
But it’s very special to us.

00:00:24.205 --> 00:00:26.371
The Sun makes life on Earth possible.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.375
Cultures across the world,
and throughout the ages,

00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:30.292
worshipped the sun.

00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:33.809
In some ways, modern life
has cut us off from the Sun.

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:37.351
In the developed world we spend
90% of our time indoors –

00:00:37.705 --> 00:00:41.913
and yet the Sun is still a powerful
force underpinning our lives.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.919
The Sun affects our perception
of beauty.

00:00:45.654 --> 00:00:47.695
When people talk of the
“golden hour”,

00:00:47.915 --> 00:00:51.165
that time just before the Sun
sets, it’s a real thing.

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:55.576
The Sun isn’t actually yellow
or orange or even red.

00:00:55.906 --> 00:00:57.865
It’s all the colours mixed together.

00:00:58.613 --> 00:01:02.322
When the Sun rises or sets
in the sky, the shortwave colours -

00:01:02.322 --> 00:01:04.072
green, blue and violet -

00:01:04.072 --> 00:01:04.947
are scattered,

00:01:04.947 --> 00:01:07.530
leaving only the yellow and
the red part of the spectrum,

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.239
giving that amazing glow.

00:01:10.135 --> 00:01:13.676
When the Sun is high in the sky,
its blue waves bounce around –

00:01:13.818 --> 00:01:15.776
which is why the sky looks blue.

00:01:16.187 --> 00:01:17.771
And when you see a rainbow,

00:01:17.771 --> 00:01:19.604
that’s the light
from the sun separated

00:01:19.604 --> 00:01:21.604
into all its magnificent colours.

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:26.523
Although we talk of the Sun rising
in the east and setting in the west,

00:01:26.523 --> 00:01:27.940
that’s not quite true.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.023
It just seems that way to us.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:32.148
The Sun stays in the same place.

00:01:32.148 --> 00:01:34.814
It’s the Earth
that rotates on its axis.

00:01:35.172 --> 00:01:38.714
This movement of the Sun is deeply
embedded in our biology.

00:01:39.531 --> 00:01:41.072
When sunlight enters the eye,

00:01:41.072 --> 00:01:43.988
it sends signals
to a master clock in our brain

00:01:43.988 --> 00:01:46.571
called the suprachiasmatic nucleus.

00:01:46.571 --> 00:01:48.780
This internal clock
regulates everything,

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.447
from when we sleep
to how we digest a meal.

00:01:52.014 --> 00:01:54.222
Messing with this
finely-tuned machine,

00:01:54.222 --> 00:01:56.805
when we work night shifts
or fly across the world,

00:01:56.805 --> 00:01:58.680
can make us feel pretty rough.

00:01:59.122 --> 00:02:02.455
Even the bluish light from a mobile
phone late at night

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:05.830
is enough to disrupt
and confuse our internal body clock.

00:02:06.188 --> 00:02:09.022
Being out of step with the Sun
affects our mood

00:02:09.022 --> 00:02:11.022
and our ability to think clearly.

00:02:11.022 --> 00:02:11.939
And there’s evidence

00:02:11.939 --> 00:02:15.356
that this kind of disruption
can lead to higher rates of obesity,

00:02:15.356 --> 00:02:18.440
heart disease,
diabetes and even cancer.

00:02:19.663 --> 00:02:21.496
We’re living out of step with the Sun

00:02:21.496 --> 00:02:25.163
and some scientists say this could
be causing a public health crisis.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.287
When the sun is shining,

00:02:27.287 --> 00:02:29.621
studies have shown that
we tip more generously

00:02:29.621 --> 00:02:32.329
and are more likely
to splash out on luxury goods.

00:02:32.896 --> 00:02:34.521
And crime may go down too.

00:02:35.104 --> 00:02:36.521
One study in the US

00:02:36.521 --> 00:02:39.146
found that when the clocks
go forward for Daylight Savings Time,

00:02:39.146 --> 00:02:43.271
the number of robberies, rapes
and murders went down around 50%.

00:02:45.412 --> 00:02:47.746
The Sun is a hell of a force
to be reckoned with.

00:02:47.746 --> 00:02:48.704
Every hour,

00:02:48.704 --> 00:02:52.204
enough sunlight falls on the
earth to power the world for a year.

00:02:55.072 --> 00:02:59.155
Deep in its core, at around
15 million degrees Celsius,

00:02:59.155 --> 00:03:02.696
the Sun is constantly fusing hydrogen
together to make helium.

00:03:02.696 --> 00:03:05.696
These nuclear reactions
release vast amounts of energy

00:03:05.696 --> 00:03:07.154
in the form of sunlight.

00:03:07.154 --> 00:03:09.904
If only we could work out
how to harness this.

00:03:10.728 --> 00:03:12.019
But we are getting there.

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:14.435
It’s estimated that by 2022,

00:03:14.435 --> 00:03:17.435
30% of our energy could
come from renewables,

00:03:17.435 --> 00:03:19.851
with solar growing faster
than anything else.

00:03:22.328 --> 00:03:24.453
The Sun is vast, dynamic…

00:03:24.453 --> 00:03:26.078
and sometimes violent.

00:03:26.078 --> 00:03:29.828
Viewed up close, the Sun’s surface
looks like a raging sea of fire,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.125
with huge eruptions

00:03:31.125 --> 00:03:34.250
rising hundreds of thousands
of kilometres into space.

00:03:34.653 --> 00:03:38.236
There are even earthquakes
on the Sun – called sunquakes.

00:03:39.237 --> 00:03:41.654
And what happens on the Sun
can impact us.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.477
The Sun sporadically blasts huge
clouds of charged particles

00:03:45.477 --> 00:03:46.769
towards the Earth,

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:50.353
potentially wreaking havoc on the
technology that we all rely on.

00:03:50.353 --> 00:03:54.020
But 97% of people have never
heard of solar storms.

00:03:54.694 --> 00:03:58.861
In 1859,
a gigantic solar storm hit the Earth,

00:03:58.861 --> 00:04:01.611
creating auroras that
covered the entire planet,

00:04:01.611 --> 00:04:03.778
knocking out the telegraph network

00:04:03.778 --> 00:04:06.653
and sending sparks flying
from electrical equipment.

00:04:06.653 --> 00:04:10.444
The solar storm of 1859
is still the largest on record.

00:04:10.444 --> 00:04:13.152
But if a similar storm
were to hit the Earth today,

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:17.311
the consequences for our way
of life could be devastating,

00:04:17.311 --> 00:04:18.769
knocking out electricity grids,

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:21.060
satellite navigation
and communications

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.227
for days, weeks…

00:04:22.227 --> 00:04:24.086
or even months.

00:04:24.086 --> 00:04:26.461
No wonder people used
to worship the sun.

